# Windows Event Logs

> Module: Hack The Box Academy – SOC Analyst Path

## Overview

This module introduced the fundamentals of Windows Event Logs and their role in security monitoring and incident response. I learned how Windows records system, application, and security events, and how analysts can use these logs to investigate suspicious activity.

---

## Learning Objectives

After completing this module, I can:

- Explain the purpose of Windows Event Logs.
- Navigate Windows Event Viewer.
- Identify the primary Windows log categories.
- Understand the structure of an event log.
- Use Event IDs during investigations.
- Recognize common Windows security events relevant to threat detection.

---

## Key Concepts

### Windows Log Categories

The primary Windows logs include:

- Application
- Security
- System
- Setup
- Forwarded Events

---

### Event Structure

Each event contains valuable investigation data:

- Event ID
- Source
- Severity Level
- Timestamp
- User
- Computer Name
- Event Details (XML)

Understanding these fields helps analysts quickly identify suspicious behavior.

---

### Event Correlation

Analysts can connect multiple related events using identifiers such as:

- Logon ID
- Process ID
- User Account
- Timestamp

This provides a clearer timeline of activity during an investigation and shifts the focus away from viewing single events in isolation.

---

## Event IDs Worth Remembering

Some Windows Event IDs that I found particularly useful include:

| Event ID | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| 4624 | Successful Logon |
| 4625 | Failed Logon |
| 4672 | Special Privileges Assigned |
| 4698 | Scheduled Task Created |
| 4719 | Audit Policy Changed |
| 7045 | Service Installed |

These events frequently appear during Windows security investigations.

